	abstract = {TD.$\lambda$/ is a popular family of algorithms for approximate policy evaluation in large MDPs. TD.$\lambda$/ works by incrementally updating the value function after each observed transition. It has two major drawbacks: it may make inefficient use of data, and it requires the user to manually tune a stepsize schedule for good performance. For the case of linear value function approximations and $\lambda$ = 0, the Least-Squares TD (LSTD) algorithm of Bradtke and Barto (1996, Machine learning, 22:1--3, 33--57) eliminates all stepsize parameters and improves data efficiency.},
